// data/exerciseLibrary.js
//
// The exercises the training engine picks from: each with the muscles it works (primary and secondary), the equipment it needs, its
// movement pattern, how hard it is to start, and, for the bodyweight progressions, where it sits on its ladder. Read it through
// lib/exerciseLibrary.js, which adds the flags worked out from these fields and finds an exercise from the name the AI or a program
// gives it. Muscles are the 17 in lib/muscleVocabulary.js; equipment, patterns and levels are the lists in lib/exerciseLibrary.js, and
// __tests__/exerciseLibrary.test.js fails if an entry uses anything outside them or leaves a field out.
//
// Where it came from. The starting list is free-exercise-db (https://github.com/yuhonas/free-exercise-db), released under the Unlicense,
// which puts it in the public domain, so nothing here needs a notice. About four in five entries are its exercises, with the muscles,
// equipment and level read through by hand rather than copied: its muscle names were mapped onto our 17, and wherever its primary
// muscle looked wrong for the movement it was changed. The rest were written for this app, mostly the rungs of the bodyweight ladders
// that the source does not have. `source` says which; `sourceId` is the entry's id in free-exercise-db, null for ours. This file is the
// copy that counts: change it here, by hand, and the tests check the result.
//
// Fields:
//   id          lowercase words joined by hyphens; never changes once the app has stored it somewhere
//   name        what the person sees
//   aliases     other names people and the AI use for the same exercise, lowercase; no two exercises share one
//   primary     the muscles that do the work (at least one); secondary the ones that help (can be empty); never the same muscle in both
//   equipment   everything the exercise needs; 'bodyweight' means it can be done with no equipment at all
//   pattern     horizontal_push, vertical_push, horizontal_pull, vertical_pull, squat, hinge, lunge, isolation, core, carry, conditioning
//   difficulty  beginner, intermediate or advanced: how hard it is to start, not how heavy it can get
//   ladder      for bodyweight progressions: { name, rung }, rung 1 the easiest, rungs counting up with no gap; null for everything else
//
// Not here, on purpose: joints and injuries. Which exercises are unsafe with which injury is a separate list, kept with the rule that uses it.

